.SH "Constructor & Destructor Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELL> \fBcsmp::NumIntegral_NT_mixed_op_dNi_dV\fP< dim, CELL >::NumIntegral_NT_mixed_op_dNi_dV (const \fBPropertyDatabase\fP< dim > & pref, const char * nodal_mtrl_multiplier, const char * oper, const char * mtrl, const char * test)"
The operand defines the fluid density, and the mtrl variable would for instance be the hydraulic conductivity\&. 
.SH "Member Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELL> void \fBcsmp::NumIntegral_NT_mixed_op_dNi_dV\fP< dim, CELL >::ComputeContribution (const CELL< dim > & e)\fR [final]\fP, \fR [override]\fP, \fR [virtual]\fP"
Computes, for example the body force term ensuing from the density field within the element\&. 
.br

.PP
This involves numerical integration over the element volume of the product:

.PP
mtrl * volume integral (NT nmult NT op) dNi

.PP
Here, the op (\fBOperand\fP) is the fluid density and 'nmult' is the nodal property which is used as material multiplier\&. 
.br
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
A reference to the finite-element from which the contribution is computed\&. 
.PP
Reimplemented from \fBcsmp::MathOperatorRHS< dim, Element >\fP\&.
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELL> void \fBcsmp::NumIntegral_NT_mixed_op_dNi_dV\fP< dim, CELL >::GetOperands (const CELL< dim > & e)\fR [final]\fP, \fR [override]\fP, \fR [virtual]\fP"
Reads the \fBOperand\fP from either the element or the nodes\&. Reads the material property from the element, and its multipliers from the nodes for later interpolation to the integration points\&. 
.br

.PP
If the MathOperator is used in a transient calculation, the function \fBMaterialPropertyTimeMultiplier()\fP allows to multiply the material parameter with the time-icrement to achieve symmetry with the lefthand side of the equation\&. This is done, for instance, if the \fBMathOperatorRHS\fP is used to compute the hydrostatic pressure contribution to a transient system\&. Do not use MultiplyWithTimeIncrement() in this case, since the acceleration of gravity must not be multiplied with delta t\&. 
.PP
Reimplemented from \fBcsmp::MathOperatorRHS< dim, Element >\fP\&.
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELL> void \fBcsmp::NumIntegral_NT_mixed_op_dNi_dV\fP< dim, CELL >::MaterialPropertyTimeMultiplier (double time_increment)"
do not use MultiplyWithTimeIncrement() here since this would multiply the whole contribution 

